Both would have made the suite silently vacuous:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. libtest writes its own progress lines to fd 1 from the main thread while running tests in parallel. Since capturing the library's output requires redirecting fd 1 (process-global), that output corrupted the captures and produced five phantom "divergences". Switched the test target to [38;5;10mharness = false[0m with a serial runner that only writes to stdout while fd 1 is un-redirected.[0m[0m
2. The crate is [38;5;10mcdylib[0m-only, so the integration test has no link dependency on it and [38;5;10mcargo test[0m does [1mnot[22m rebuild the [38;5;10m.so[0m. My first negative control passed [3mwith a sign-extension bug injected[23m because it loaded a stale library. Added an mtime freshness guard that hard-fails, and made the runner script build explicitly first.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I then confirmed the suite is not vacuous with five negative controls, each rebuilt properly: sign-extension ([38;5;10mbyte as i8[0m, 20/31 fail), big-endian byte order (26 fail), dropped trailing newline (29 fail), uppercase [38;5;10m%02X[0m (25 fail), and dropped zero-pad [38;5;10m%x[0m (26 fail). The staleness guard also fires on demand. [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m was restored byte-identically afterward, and nothing in [38;5;10mc_src/[0m was modified — only [38;5;10mc_src/build/[0m was created.[0m[0m
